course: dictionary
lesson: 6415
title: The mussel: a shellfish in two halves
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mussel is a shellfish that lives in the sea or in rivers. Its shell is long and dark blue or black, and it opens into two halves, like a little book.
explain: Mussels stick to rocks with strong threads. They stay in one place and filter the water to take in their food, so they do not chase their dinner.
explain: «A sea star opens a mussel by pulling with its tube feet until the mussel tires.» Here the starfish is the hunter and the mussel is its meal. People also eat mussels, cooked until their shells open.
fact: A mussel is a sea or river shellfish with a long dark blue or black shell in two halves.
fact: «Freshwater mussels filter and clean river water as they feed.»
fact: «Pearl mussels can live over 100 years.»
fact: «Pearls are made by oysters and mussels.»
fact: Mussels are cooked and eaten as food, and they are ready when their shells open.
exercise: «A sea star opens a mussel by pulling with its tube feet.» What is a mussel? | *A sea or river shellfish with a dark shell in two halves | A kind of starfish | A type of seaweed | A small brown bird
exercise: What colour is a mussel's shell? | Bright red and spotted | *Long and dark blue or black | White and flat | Green and glassy
exercise: Mussels stick to rocks with strong threads. How do mussels stay on rocks? | With long legs | *With strong threads | By swimming away | By growing roots
test: Where do mussels live? | Only in deserts | Only on land | *In the sea or in rivers | Only in rivers
test: Why do people eat mussels? | To make the sea warmer | *Because they are cooked and eaten as food | Because they have a brain | To make a pearl
exam: Which sentence describes a mussel correctly? | A fish with fins and scales | *A shellfish with a long dark shell in two halves | A plant that grows on the sea floor | A bird that swims in lakes
